> Tim May writes:
> > (Obviously I am not calculating my rate of return on the original
> > purchase price. I can explain why if anybody doesn't know why.)
> 
> I "knew" that you wouldn't calculate it that way, but I can't remember
> why. Care to elaborate?
> 
> Elias Israel

Let me give a simple example, which is better than a bunch of
equations and the like.

Suppose I own a home in Palo Alto, California that I bought in 1970
for $25,000. 

I am now renting it out for $2000 a month, or $24,000 a year. Is it
proper to call my rate of return 100%? Of course not.

Those who bought houses in Palo Alto in 1970 for $25,000 and can now
sell them for $400,000 did extremely well, but this is not the same
thing as the return on rental property.

One must always use the _current_ fair market price, not what one
originally paid. (Harry Browne cites this mode of thinking, of past
costs, as one of the main traps people fall into.)

By the way, the ROI (return on investment) in areas like Palo Alto is
truly abysmal. That $400,000 house might rent for $2000 - $2200 a month, but
probably less once the vacancy rate and property taxes are taken into
account (prop tax = $7000 a year = $580 a month). I figure the ROI to
be around 4.5% before taxes and inflation. And with the considerable
downside risk that the house you buy for $400,000 today will sell for
$350,000 in two years. (Of course, it might also go up, which is why
the instantaneous rate of return needs to be kept compartmentalized
from the issued of capital gain or loss, except as it enters the risk
side of the calculations.)

I wanted to add another comment about Dani Eder's situation. The
Seattle market that he is in is doing very well, as I'm sure you have
all heard. Vacancy rates are low, house prices are presumably still
rising.

Again, the transient ups and downs of a particular market are not the
same thing as expected ROI on rentals.

--Tim May


