Tim Mac writes:

>Oh, come on, Nancy! These are anecdotal reports of people who are rare
>exceptions. The four sigma folks. Most illiterates don't have
>companies, and most smart people don't remain illiterate. That there
>are _some_ of these folks means little.

How do you know this? I've heard people say that 40% of Americans are  
functionally illiterate (they read below fifth grade level). I can
imagine that there are tens if not hundreds of thousands of people in
America who do not read or even *speak* English very well and carry on
thriving businesses.

And as to the point that smart people don't remain illiterate - that
may be true. But perhaps many of those people become literate only
because they *have* to, in order to get the amount of sophisticated
knowledge they need to accomplish their goals. All of the emerging
technologies we are seeing today may be changing that. If you can learn,
record, and exchange knowledge easily and quickly without interpreting
most of it into alphanumeric code (ie writing), then why bother? In the
near future, the statement:

"If someone wants to be smart and successful,
they'll need to know how to read." 


may be as ridiculous as the statement,

"If someone *really* wants to be smart and sucessful,
they'll need to know how to do calculus (or read sheet music)."

is today. Yes, it will definitely be an advantage in many(most?) fields
to be able to read. But probably in fewer fields than today, and that
number will probably continue to decrease.

>The recent surge in so-called homeless, at least here in the People's
>Republic, is not promising. Most of them have been "homeless" (bums,
>winos, living in homeless shelters, sleeping under bridges) for only a
>few years, but show no signs of wanting to clean themselves up.

Again, what I have heard/read from various sources is that the large
majority of adult homeless are *not* substance abusers. And that most
of those who are homeless or live below the poverty level are in fact
children. But most people who are without a permanent address do *not*
hang out on street corners begging change. They stay out of sight, in
shelters or wherever they can, probably because in this country if you
are poor you are reviled, and it is only the truly wasted or sociopathic
who can withstand the public aprobrium.

Clearly, this difference in opinion about the state of homelessness is best
addressed by tracking down some reliable citations. I will attempt to do so.

